<!doctype html>
<html lang="no">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
{#- Keeps a github.io mirror (PATH_PREFIX=/javazone-calendar/) from competing
    with the custom domain in search: both build the same page, so without
    this a crawler sees two URLs for identical content and has to guess which
    one to rank. #}
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ page.url | absUrl }}">
{#- Open Graph and Twitter card, so a link pasted into Slack, LinkedIn,
    iMessage or Discord unfurls into a card instead of a bare URL. One
    twitter:* tag is enough - Twitter itself falls back to og:* for title,
    description and image, and every other reader here only looks at og:*
    in the first place. og:title is the page's own share title rather than
    <title>: og:site_name already names the site once, so the title need
    not say it again. og:locale is nb_NO unconditionally - the EN toggle in
    app.js is client-side only, so a crawler only ever sees the Norwegian
    page. #}
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:site_name" content="JavaZone 2026 — program">
<meta property="og:locale" content="nb_NO">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ page.url | absUrl }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ shareTitle }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ shareDescription }}">
{#- The same card in two formats, offered as repeated og:image tags. There is
    no content negotiation to lean on here: GitHub Pages serves static files
    and does not vary on Accept, and <picture> cannot help because no page
    element ever renders a card - the only thing that ever reads one is a
    crawler reading these tags. A repeated og:image is the one choice Open
    Graph itself provides.

    PNG first and WebP second, and that order is the whole decision. A consumer
    that reads the list picks the format it prefers; most take the first image
    they see, and those - LinkedIn, Slack, iMessage - have to land on the
    format that decodes everywhere.

    Each og:image is immediately followed by its own :type, :width, :height and
    :alt, because a structured property attaches to the most recent og:image
    above it. Interleaving the two groups would silently describe each image
    with the other's properties, so the two blocks below stay whole.

    A session sets shareImage and shareImageWebp to its own card
    (program.11tydata.js); everything else, the day grids included, unfurls
    with the site-wide one. Every card is 1200x630 in both formats, so the
    dimensions hold either way - they are here so an unfurler can reserve the
    space before the image arrives. The alt text is bound once and set twice
    because it describes the picture, and it is the same picture. The site-wide
    card's own description is built from the words scripts/build-og-card.mjs
    draws on it, so the two cannot drift apart. #}
{%- set shareAlt = shareImageAlt or ("Mørkeblå plakat med JavaZone-logoen og teksten " + site.event.name + ", Program, " + site.event.dateRange.no + ".") %}
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ (shareImage or '/icons/og.png') | absUrl }}">
<meta property="og:image:type" content="image/png">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ shareAlt }}">
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ (shareImageWebp or '/icons/og.webp') | absUrl }}">
<meta property="og:image:type" content="image/webp">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ shareAlt }}">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#153863">
<meta name="app-version" content="{{ site.version }}">
{#- The programme hash above changes only when the programme does. This one
    changes when anything shipped does - templates, CSS, JS, speaker photos -
    and it is what names the service worker's cache, so it is the honest
    answer to "is this page the build the server is serving". #}
<meta name="app-build" content="{{ site.buildId }}">
<meta name="base-path" content="{{ "/" | url }}">
<link rel="manifest" href="{{ "/manifest.webmanifest" | url }}">
{#- Two favicons rather than the one scalable file that used to be here, because
    the mark is now a raster mascot. A browser given a single icon downscales
    it, and Duke taken from 512 down to 16 is a smudge with a red dot in it -
    so each of these is drawn at the size it is actually shown at, from a crop
    chosen for that size. See scripts/build-icons.mjs. #}
<link rel="icon" href="{{ "/icons/icon-32.png" | url }}" sizes="32x32" type="image/png">
<link rel="icon" href="{{ "/icons/icon-16.png" | url }}" sizes="16x16" type="image/png">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="{{ "/icons/icon-180.png" | url }}">
{#- The build id on the stylesheet and script URLs is what lets a fresh page
    carry fresh assets. The worker serves these cache-first under their URL,
    so without it a page fetched past the caches (the update button, or a new
    worker's precache) would still be styled by whatever the previous worker
    had stored - and iOS Safari can keep a previous worker around for a long
    time after a deploy. A new URL is a cache miss in any worker. #}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ "/css/fonts.css" | url }}?v={{ site.buildId }}">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ "/css/style.css" | url }}?v={{ site.buildId }}">
{#- After the stylesheets, deliberately. A preload is fetched at the highest
    priority there is, and these fonts are not needed for the first paint at
    all - font-display: swap paints the text in a fallback immediately. Ahead
    of the render-blocking CSS they simply queued the typography in front of
    the few KB that actually gates painting, which Safari does not reorder the
    way Chrome does. Only the four weights every page uses are listed: 500 and
    italic 400 appear on one view each and can arrive late. #}
{%- for f in ["montserrat-400", "montserrat-600", "montserrat-700", "montserrat-800"] %}
<link rel="preload" href="{{ ("/css/fonts/" + f + "-latin.woff2") | url }}" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
{%- endfor %}
<script defer src="{{ "/js/rum.js" | url }}?v={{ site.buildId }}"></script>
</head>
<body>
{{ content | safe }}

<script type="module" src="{{ "/js/app.js" | url }}?v={{ site.buildId }}"></script>
</body>
</html>
